# Line-ending safeguard for byte-anchored artifacts. This is an integrity
# control, not a formatting preference.
#
# Why: this archive is written from Windows, where core.autocrlf=true rewrites
# checked-out files to CRLF. For ordinary text that is harmless. For the files
# below it is not:
#
#   * ledger.jsonl is hash-chained and append-only. A CRLF commit would rewrite
#     the recorded bytes of entries that are supposed to be frozen.
#   * Locked card.json / meta.json are hashed into the ledger and their SHA-256
#     is published. One converted byte invalidates the published hash.
#   * .ots files are OpenTimestamps proofs anchored to Bitcoin. They prove a
#     specific byte sequence existed at a specific time. If the file those
#     proofs cover changes by even one byte, the proof no longer verifies --
#     silently, and in a way that looks like tampering rather than a checkout
#     artifact.
#
# Caught 2026-07-25 in the sibling `edge-card` repo, where a checkout left the
# shadow ledger with 8 CRLF endings while the committed blob had none. This
# archive was clean at the time (0 CRLF), so nothing needed repair -- but the
# hazard is identical and the consequences here are worse, because these bytes
# are externally anchored and publicly hashed.
#
# Deliberately NARROW. Existing archive content is not renormalised and no
# `git add --renormalize .` was run: that would rewrite exactly the bytes this
# file exists to protect.

# -text disables EOL conversion in BOTH directions, so the working tree always
# holds exactly the committed bytes.
# Generated site + source text may normalise to LF safely.
*.html   text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf

# Byte-anchored artifacts. These come LAST: in .gitattributes the final matching
# line wins, so listing them after the general *.txt rule is what makes HASH.txt
# actually protected rather than silently normalised.
ledger.jsonl        -text
*.jsonl             -text
*.json              -text
*.ots               -text
*.sha256            -text
HASH.txt            -text

# Binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
